/* combos-work.js — classic script; assigns to window.TD. No ES modules, so file:// works.
   What two cards say together in a reading about work, career and money. For now it covers the 231 pairs of two
   Major Arcana cards, keyed "idA|idB" in deck order like combos.js (see TD.pairKey in js/spreads.js);
   any other pair shows the general reading from combos.js, labelled "General reading".
   Written fresh for this demo; a live site edits or replaces them. Loaded only when a reading needs it. */
window.TD = window.TD || {};
window.TD.COMBOS_WORK = {"major-00|major-01":"Newness and know-how are lined up on the same desk. Volunteer for the project nobody has done before, since you have the tools to figure it out. Ask for a small trial run and show what you built instead of describing it. A working sample persuades faster than a long explanation.",
"major-00|major-02":"A new direction may be forming in you before you can defend it in a meeting. Keep your impressions to yourself for a week and jot them down, then compare them with the facts. Do not announce a switch until you can explain it in one clear sentence to a skeptic.",
"major-00|major-03":"A fresh venture needs nourishment as much as ambition. Look at how you feed the new effort: sleep, decent meals, a workspace you like, colleagues who are kind. Build those in from week one. Work that comes from a cared-for person tends to last longer than work fueled by adrenaline.",
"major-00|major-04":"A bold idea meets a boss, a deadline or a set of limits, and the meeting is useful. Bring your pitch in a form that respects what is already decided: scope, timeline, who approves what. Ask about the frame before you present the picture. Ideas that arrive with structure get taken seriously.",
"major-00|major-05":"Someone experienced holds the map to a place you have never worked. Sign up for the training, take the veteran to lunch and learn the unwritten rules before you bend any. Once you know how things are done and why, your fresh angle will be welcomed as a contribution, not an interruption.",
"major-00|major-06":"A crossroads is opening, and it is less about which role sounds grander than which one fits who you are becoming. Write down what you want from work in plain terms, then hold each option against the list. Pay attention to colleagues who share your values, since that match matters daily.",
"major-00|major-07":"Raw enthusiasm has been handed a destination. Choose one goal for the next quarter, name the first milestone and trim the projects that pull you sideways. Energy is an asset only when it points somewhere. Tell a colleague your target so someone can notice when you drift.",
"major-00|major-08":"Being new is uncomfortable, and quiet courage helps more than a loud performance. When you make a beginner's mistake, own it briefly and fix it. Patience with your learning curve, and with colleagues who are slow to trust you, builds a reputation faster than trying to prove yourself in month one.",
"major-00|major-09":"A career leap looks different once you have thought about it alone with a notebook. Set aside a few evenings to ask what you want your days to feel like, and write plain answers. Talk to a trusted mentor afterward, once you know your own mind, and then choose.",
"major-00|major-10":"A reshuffle, a shift in circumstances or an unexpected opening may arrive on a schedule that is not yours. Keep your skills and contacts fresh so you can say yes quickly. Avoid pinning all your hopes on one outcome, and treat this as one turn of a longer cycle.",
"major-00|major-11":"A fresh start comes with fine print, and reading it now spares you frustration later. Ask about terms, expectations and how performance is judged before you commit. Treat colleagues evenly, keep your promises and write down what was agreed. A beginning built on clear, fair terms holds up when things get busy.",
"major-00|major-12":"You are eager to move, and the situation asks you to wait and look at it differently. Pause a pending decision for a few days and describe the problem as a customer, boss or rival would. A new angle may turn a leap into a smarter step, or reveal a better door.",
"major-00|major-13":"A role, routine or way of picturing your career has run its course, and the space it leaves is workable. Clear your desk, refresh your materials and say a proper goodbye to the old identity. Then try one small experiment in a new direction, and allow yourself to be a beginner again.",
"major-00|major-14":"Enthusiasm is more useful once it is measured out. Introduce your fresh approach in small portions alongside what already works, and watch how colleagues respond before adding more. A new role takes shape over weeks, not days, so find a working rhythm you can actually sustain.",
"major-00|major-15":"Something keeps you tied to a job, routine or belief about your worth, and a fresh option is asking you to test that hold. List what genuinely keeps you where you are, then sort each item into real obligation or habit. Take one small step that loosens the least essential tie.",
"major-00|major-16":"A surprise, such as a reorganization, a lost project or a sudden change of plan, can sting and free you at once. Take a day to absorb it, then list the skills and relationships that are still yours. Begin from what is real and be willing to build differently this time.",
"major-00|major-17":"Renewed hope makes a fresh professional start feel possible, even if recent months were discouraging. Aim at a modest, honest goal you can share, such as learning a skill or offering help where it is needed. Steady, generous effort builds a reputation more surely than a flashy announcement.",
"major-00|major-18":"The path ahead is not fully visible, so gather more information before committing. Ask colleagues plain questions, read the details of any offer twice and hold off on irreversible moves until the picture sharpens. Your instinct may be right, but for now it is a hunch that deserves checking.",
"major-00|major-19":"Your enthusiasm is contagious and the timing is good. Share your new project openly and let colleagues see how much you enjoy it. Ask for feedback while the mood is bright. Good work is easier to do when people can see you like it, so do not hide the pleasure.",
"major-00|major-20":"Your work history is calling for an honest review so you can answer a new opportunity from a stronger place. Write down what you have done well, what you have outgrown and what you still want to try. Then apply, ask or pitch for the role that fits who you are now.",
"major-00|major-21":"A chapter of your work has come to a satisfying end, and that is what frees you to start something else. Close out loose tasks, thank the people who helped and note what you achieved. Then, with your record in hand, open a new project or role, knowing you are not starting from zero.",
"major-01|major-02":"Skill and instinct are teamed up. Do the research, then check it against your gut sense about the people and the project. If the facts say yes and the room feels off, look further. Act on your read of the situation, and use the tools you have, not every tool available.",
"major-01|major-03":"A project benefits when skill is combined with patient care. Choose work that grows with steady attention and give it regular time each day. Look after your energy, your team and your workspace, since a nurtured effort tends to bear more fruit than a forced one. Let your craft develop at a natural pace.",
"major-01|major-04":"Talent needs a framework to produce results. Set a schedule, define clear goals and agree on who decides what. Use your inventive streak inside those lines instead of arguing with them. Ask a person with authority to hold you to your timeline, and deliver something finished, not just clever.",
"major-01|major-05":"Learn the established method before you improve on it. Find a mentor, a recognized course or a respected practitioner and study how the craft is done properly. Once you understand why the rules exist, you can bend them with purpose. Respect for tradition makes your original ideas more persuasive to people in charge.",
"major-01|major-06":"Two job paths may both be within reach, and your skills would fit either one. Before you commit, write down what you want your working days to feel like in a year, then pick the role that serves that list. Talent is not the question here. Fit is.",
"major-01|major-07":"Your resources finally have a destination. Choose two or three priorities and put your tools behind them. Tell a colleague your target and hold the reins firmly so scattered demands cannot pull you sideways. Progress comes from steady direction and focus, not from adding more tools.",
"major-01|major-08":"Real mastery is often quiet. Handle a tense colleague or an unruly project with calm, patient skill instead of forceful display. Do the small, skillful thing well and repeat it. A steady presence and a gentle hand tend to win people over, and a reputation for composure lasts longer than one for flash.",
"major-01|major-09":"Take your tools somewhere quiet and ask one honest question about your craft: what do I actually want to make? A stretch of focused, solitary work will show you things a busy calendar hides. Come back when you have an answer, and share it with someone whose opinion you respect.",
"major-01|major-10":"Preparation and chance shake hands. Keep your portfolio updated, your skills sharp and your contacts warm so that a sudden turn finds you ready. Put effort into what you control and let go of the rest. If the timing does not favor you this round, your readiness still belongs to you.",
"major-01|major-11":"Good work is about to be measured fairly. Keep clean records of what you built, what it cost and who helped, because a review, contract or pay conversation will go better with evidence on the table. Skill gets you noticed; being straight about the numbers gets you paid.",
"major-01|major-12":"Set your tools down for a few days and look at the problem from the other side. Imagine how a customer, a critic or a coworker sees it, and note what changes. A willing pause often produces a simpler and kinder next move than another burst of effort. Let the delay work for you.",
"major-01|major-13":"A project, role or income stream is finishing, and your abilities are not leaving with it. List the tools, contacts and know-how you are carrying out the door. Some of them belong in the next thing, and some only made sense in the old setup. Sort them before you start again.",
"major-01|major-14":"Steady progress beats a big launch right now. Put your best effort into a routine you can keep for months: a set number of hours, a fixed budget line, one skill practiced weekly. Mixing ambition with patience is slower, but it holds up when the first excitement wears off.",
"major-01|major-15":"Watch where your talent is being spent. A job, client or habit may be using your ability in ways that pay but leave you stuck, or you may be using clever shortcuts that are starting to cost more than they save. Name the trade you are making, then decide if it is still worth it.",
"major-01|major-16":"Something at work may give way without much warning: a plan, a structure, an arrangement that looked solid. Your ability to adapt is the real asset here. Rebuild from what is actually true now, not from the old org chart, and you may find a better fit than the one that fell.",
"major-01|major-17":"A sense that things can improve is joined by the skill to help them along. Put real effort into repair instead of waiting for it, by resuming a paused project, mending a working relationship or tidying a neglected process. Small, careful acts of restoration carry more weight than they appear to.",
"major-01|major-18":"Not every offer or figure is what it seems. You have the skill to act, but details are unclear, so ask for terms in writing and check the numbers twice before you put your name on anything. Hold off on the big move until the picture sharpens a little.",
"major-01|major-19":"Your work is in full light, with energy, warmth and honest pleasure in it. Show it to people who will enjoy it, and do not dress it up with false modesty. Ask for the recognition your results have earned. Being visible and cheerful about your skills tends to bring more of the work you enjoy.",
"major-01|major-20":"Your track record is being looked at, possibly by you. An appraisal, a portfolio refresh or an honest look back at the last few years could show which skills people really rely on. Answer that call by putting those skills forward and letting go of work that no longer reflects you.",
"major-01|major-21":"A long piece of work is ready to be finished and shown. Tie up the loose ends, ship it and let people see the whole thing. Completing this cycle well puts your abilities on record and opens the door to a bigger stage, possibly one that reaches further than your current circle.",
"major-02|major-03":"Your quiet read on a project is worth trusting, and it needs feeding to grow. Give the hunch time and resources: a test run, a notebook of observations, a week of steady effort. Ideas at work often ripen when someone tends them patiently instead of announcing them early.",
"major-02|major-04":"Trust your read of the room, then build a system around it. Perhaps you sense a process is off, or a colleague is holding something back. Write down what you notice, propose one simple rule or checkpoint, and let the structure do the speaking for you.",
"major-02|major-05":"What you sense privately and what the workplace teaches may point in different directions. Learn the official way well enough to know where your instinct genuinely adds something. A mentor or a training course can help, as long as you keep your own judgment in the room.",
"major-02|major-06":"A decision about who or what to commit to is best made from values, not pressure. Sit with the options in silence before you compare pay or titles. Ask which one you would still respect in a year, then tell a trusted colleague your answer out loud.",
"major-02|major-07":"Momentum is available, but your gut is asking for a moment before you press ahead. Check the direction before the speed: is this the goal you actually want? Spend an hour on quiet planning, then commit and move with confidence. Work goes further when the driver knows the route.",
"major-02|major-08":"Stay steady with a tricky person or task and let calm do what force cannot. You sense more than you say, which is useful in a tense meeting. Hold your tone, listen longer than feels natural, and let the quieter approach win people over gradually.",
"major-02|major-09":"Step back from the noise and let a problem come clear. Block time without meetings, ask one honest question, and write down whatever surfaces. A colleague who sees you working more quietly is not necessarily seeing you disengaged. The answer likely comes from you, once you make space.",
"major-02|major-10":"Circumstances at work are turning, and your instinct picked up on it early. Use that head start calmly: quietly update your skills, tidy your records, and stay flexible. You cannot control a reorganization or a market mood, but you can meet it prepared.",
"major-02|major-11":"Facts and intuition need to agree before you act. If something feels off about a contract, a review, or how credit is being shared, check the records and the wording. Trust the hunch enough to ask questions, and trust the paperwork enough to hear the answers.",
"major-02|major-12":"Stalled progress may be a gift for now. While the project sits, turn your view around: what would the client, the newest hire or the skeptic see? Use the delay to gather insight rather than pushing. The next step will make more sense after you have looked properly.",
"major-02|major-13":"A chapter of your work life is closing, and the inner knowing has been there a while. Say it to yourself plainly, then take small tidy steps: finish loose ends, hand off what you can, and leave space before filling it. Endings handled well make new starts possible.",
"major-02|major-14":"The middle of a long project is where quiet judgment matters most. Balance workload, energy and relationships instead of sprinting. Trust your sense of what is enough for one week, and adjust as you go. Steady, measured effort will serve you better than heroics.",
"major-02|major-15":"You suspect a routine or a dynamic has more hold on you than it deserves. Whether it is the comfort of a title, a difficult boss or an unhealthy pace, take your private doubts seriously. Write down what it costs you, then choose one small step toward more freedom.",
"major-02|major-16":"A surprise change at work may land like a fire alarm, yet part of you was half prepared. Do the immediate practical things first: read the messages, ask what is expected, protect your records. Later, reflect on which warning signs you noticed and how to trust them sooner.",
"major-02|major-17":"Renewed confidence is growing after a rough patch, and it does not need an audience. Keep a modest, steady routine that restores your energy and skills. Small, honest progress adds up. Share your plans with people who support you and hold back from those who drain you.",
"major-02|major-18":"Not everything is visible yet, and your unease deserves attention without becoming a story. Collect facts before reacting: ask for the missing details, reread the fine print, and hold off on big commitments. What feels unclear today usually resolves when you keep looking steadily.",
"major-02|major-19":"A hunch you have kept to yourself is ready to be shared, and the room is likely to respond well. Present it clearly, without hedging, and let people see your enthusiasm. Good news at work often arrives when quiet preparation meets an open moment.",
"major-02|major-20":"You have a clear inner sense of whether your current path suits you, and it is asking for a proper look. Review your past year honestly: what you did well, what you avoided. Then respond to the pull you feel and take one concrete step.",
"major-02|major-21":"A big effort is wrapping up, and your quiet observations helped shape it. Finish properly: document what you learned, thank those who helped, and reflect on what you would do differently. Completing well gives you confidence for whatever comes next.",
"major-03|major-04":"Creativity needs a container. You may have plenty of ideas, resources or goodwill, and the job now is to give them a schedule, a budget and clear roles. Build a simple structure around what is already growing, and keep it flexible enough to nourish the work.",
"major-03|major-05":"Learning from people who came before you feeds your growth. Find a mentor, join a training or study how experienced colleagues handle their work. Then bring your own care and creativity to it. Skills passed down thoughtfully turn into something you can pass on yourself.",
"major-03|major-06":"Choose the work you are willing to nurture. Options may look equal, but one matches your values and will grow with attention. Ask which project or team you would happily tend for years. Then give that choice regular, generous effort.",
"major-03|major-07":"Resources are there and so is the drive to use them. Point the effort at one clear goal, and look after the people and energy that carry it. Steady progress needs meals, rest and encouragement. A well-fed team moves farther than a hurried one.",
"major-03|major-08":"Gentle persistence is winning here. A difficult colleague or a slow project responds better to warmth and consistency than to force. Keep showing up, keep your temper even, and reward small steps. Over time, that steady care turns into real trust and results.",
"major-03|major-09":"Step away from the busy hive to look after your own energy. A short retreat, a day without meetings or a walk alone can feed your best thinking. Return with one clear question or idea. Nourished focus beats constant output.",
"major-03|major-10":"Growth cycles come and go, and you seem to be entering a fruitful one. Prepare by looking after your resources and relationships, and keep your plans flexible. Not every good turn can be forced, but you can be ready, generous and well rested when it comes.",
"major-03|major-11":"Fair treatment and steady care go together. Look at how credit, workload and pay are shared, and raise concerns kindly with facts. A workplace that feeds its people also keeps honest books. Be generous, but keep clear records so generosity does not become resentment.",
"major-03|major-12":"A pause in progress may be feeding the project rather than starving it. Let the idea grow while you look at it from a fresh angle. Rest, ask for other views, and resist forcing a result. Sometimes a stalled season is where the deepest ideas take root.",
"major-03|major-13":"Something in your work is ending, and it is clearing room for growth. Let the old routine, role or project go with thanks. Then plant something new in the space: a skill, a contact, a smaller experiment. Composting the old makes the next season richer.",
"major-03|major-14":"Sustainable effort wins over bursts. Blend your resources, time and energy in moderate portions, and keep a pace you could hold for months. Look after your health and your team's, and make small adjustments as things change. Good work grows from an even hand.",
"major-03|major-15":"Watch for comforts that hold you in place: a familiar role, an easy perk, a habit of overworking to feel needed. Ask honestly what you are getting from it and what it costs. Then loosen your grip on one thing, and see how that feels.",
"major-03|major-16":"Something comfortable at work may be disrupted, whether a routine, a team or a plan. Protect your footing first: rest, eat, talk to allies. Then look at what remains and what you can grow from it. Rebuilding with better foundations takes time.",
"major-03|major-17":"Fresh hope is taking root after a tough period. Feed it with regular rest, orderly records and a plan you can actually keep. Tell supportive people what you are aiming for. Nurturing small, real progress builds a base of confidence for later.",
"major-03|major-18":"Plenty of potential is here, but the picture is not clear yet. Avoid locking in plans on guesses. Ask questions, request missing details, and keep your energy steady while things settle. Grounded routines protect you from worry spirals while facts come in.",
"major-03|major-19":"Good energy and real results can go together. Celebrate what is going well, thank the people who helped, and let your enthusiasm show. A cheerful, generous team culture makes hard tasks lighter. Keep enjoying the work while it is working.",
"major-03|major-20":"Take an honest look at what your efforts have produced and what they cost you. Are you nurturing the right things? Answer the pull toward change with a practical step, such as a conversation, a new routine or a project you have deferred.",
"major-03|major-21":"Sustained care has led to something finished and good. Take time to enjoy the result and thank everyone who helped. Then rest properly before the next thing. A project handled with generosity leaves a team ready and willing to work together again.",
"major-04|major-05":"Rules, hierarchy and established methods are strong forces here. Learn how the organization actually decides things and follow its proven paths while you build authority. A mentor or formal credential can help. Question outdated rules respectfully, with a better alternative in hand.",
"major-04|major-06":"A significant decision about partnership or direction needs a clear framework. List the values and non-negotiables, then compare your options honestly. Choose what fits, and back it up with written agreements. Good working relationships rest on shared principles and clear terms.",
"major-04|major-07":"Authority and momentum are on your side. Set the plan, assign the roles, and drive toward the target with confidence. Keep listening as you go so speed does not outrun sense. Leadership works best when people know where they are going and why.",
"major-04|major-08":"Lead with steady calm rather than volume. Authority comes across best when paired with patience: keep standards clear, hear people out, and correct without shaming. Difficult situations at work respond to consistent, kind firmness better than to pressure.",
"major-04|major-09":"Step away from daily management to think about the larger structure. Are the systems, roles and goals right? Take quiet time to review, jot down one honest question, and return with a simple change. Good leaders take space to see clearly.",
"major-04|major-10":"Plan carefully, then accept that conditions will move. Build a structure that can bend: buffers, backups, honest check-ins. Not every change is yours to control. Steady leadership means responding well to whatever turns up. A written plan with room for surprises holds up better than a rigid one.",
"major-04|major-11":"Rules, agreements and fairness are central. Make sure roles are defined, commitments are written clearly, and decisions follow consistent standards. Own your part of any outcome. A well-run workplace treats people evenly and keeps accurate records.",
"major-04|major-12":"A planned schedule stalls, and that may be useful. Instead of forcing the timeline, take a step back and question your assumptions. Consider how the problem looks from a customer's or a junior colleague's chair. Then adjust the structure with what you learned.",
"major-04|major-13":"A structure at work is being taken apart, and it may be time. A role, policy or leadership habit has run its course. Shut it down cleanly, document what it taught you, and design something simpler. Firm endings make room for better foundations.",
"major-04|major-14":"Combine structure with adaptability. Set firm goals, and blend methods and people so the whole runs smoothly. Pace yourself for the long haul. Steady, measured leadership, with small corrections along the way, gets more done than intense pushes.",
"major-04|major-15":"Power and ambition may be pulling harder than you think. Ask whether status, a title or the need to control has become a trap. Look at who benefits from rigid rules. Choose one habit of over-control to release, and give someone else a genuine say.",
"major-04|major-16":"A structure you relied on gets shaken, whether a plan, a leader or a policy. Steady yourself, gather facts, and do the next necessary task. Later, redesign with what the surprise revealed. Foundations built on real needs, not appearances, hold up better.",
"major-04|major-17":"A renewed sense of direction can be built into daily habits. Set modest, regular goals and protect the time for them. Hope becomes real when it has a plan. Ask for guidance from steady people, and let your confidence rebuild on evidence.",
"major-04|major-18":"Structure is being tested by unclear information. Resist rigid decisions based on rumor or worry. Keep your routines steady, gather concrete facts, and check assumptions with reliable sources. Clarity usually arrives, and calm leadership in the meantime holds people together.",
"major-04|major-19":"Solid leadership and a good result meet. Clear plans and firm standards are paying off. Share the credit, keep your structure simple, and let your confidence be visible. A well-run team that enjoys its work is a strong asset.",
"major-04|major-20":"A serious review of your leadership, goals or systems is due. Face the results honestly, own what you did, and decide what changes. Then set new standards and follow through. Accountability that starts with you tends to spread.",
"major-04|major-21":"Long, disciplined effort reaches a finish. Structures you built are working, and a major goal is met. Record how you did it, thank contributors, and take a real breather. A well-completed project becomes a template for the next one.",
"major-05|major-06":"Ethics and belonging shape a choice. An organization's traditions and your own principles may or may not match. Weigh which culture you can wholeheartedly join. Ask mentors and read the written values, then choose the place where your integrity and your role align.",
"major-05|major-07":"Proven methods and forward drive combine well. Use the established process, get the training or certification, and push toward your target with focus. Follow the rules that help and question the ones that do not. Discipline within a system can move you fast.",
"major-05|major-08":"Learn the ropes patiently and keep your temper. A mentor, a training program or an old-school colleague may test your composure. Respond with calm and curiosity. You can honor the way things are done and still quietly show a better way.",
"major-05|major-09":"Retreat from the group's opinions to hear yourself think. Study quietly, read the source material, and form your own view of how your field works. Then consult a trusted teacher for a second opinion. Independent understanding makes learning stick.",
"major-05|major-10":"Institutions change, and the trusted route may shift under you. Stay close to your principles and keep learning. Accept that promotions, rules and markets turn on their own timing. Being adaptable within a solid foundation helps.",
"major-05|major-11":"Formal rules, policies and agreements are prominent. Read the handbook and the process, and make sure you follow them and that others do too. If something is unfair, raise it through proper channels with facts. Integrity in procedure earns respect.",
"major-05|major-12":"Pausing your usual training or route can reveal a better one. Question a standard practice and consider it from an outsider's view. A delay in advancement may give you time to learn something unofficial but valuable. Return with a fresher perspective.",
"major-05|major-13":"An old institution, method or mentor relationship is coming to a close. Thank it for what it taught you, then move on cleanly. A qualification or tradition that once defined you may give way to a new path. Endings like this are a graduation.",
"major-05|major-14":"Combine established methods with your own judgment, in measured amounts. Learn from teachers, then adapt the lessons to your situation. Steady, patient practice beats cramming. A balanced approach to rules and creativity makes for dependable work.",
"major-05|major-15":"Rigid dogma or an unspoken system may be trapping you. Ask whether you follow a rule because it works or because you fear stepping out. Look at what the institution gets from your compliance. Seek an outside opinion, and test one small change.",
"major-05|major-16":"A trusted institution or method may be shaken by sudden change. Stay steady, verify what the rules now say, and ask questions. It can be unsettling when the known way fails. Afterward, you can build a more honest understanding of how things work.",
"major-05|major-17":"Guidance from a mentor or a proven method restores your confidence. Study, ask for advice, and follow a plan that has worked for others. Hope grows when you see a path. Share what you learn as you go, because teaching reinforces your own progress.",
"major-05|major-18":"Official information may be murky or contradictory. Rely on documented facts and trusted advisors, and avoid acting on rumor. Ask for clarification in writing. When the way forward is foggy, established procedures can help you keep your footing.",
"major-05|major-19":"Learning and belonging are going well. A supportive mentor, a good training program or a healthy team culture gives you confidence. Show appreciation and share what you have learned. Work that respects tradition and welcomes newcomers tends to shine.",
"major-05|major-20":"A formal evaluation, a review of your qualifications or a reconsideration of your field is in play. Be honest about what you have learned and where you have stalled. Answer the call to grow, perhaps by seeking further training or a role that fits.",
"major-05|major-21":"Study and practice culminate in a real accomplishment. A degree, certification or long project may be ready to be recognized. Take part in the ceremony, thank your teachers, and consider how to pass knowledge on. Completion brings standing.",
"major-06|major-07":"A clear decision unlocks momentum. Choose the option that reflects your values, tell the people involved, and commit your energy. Half-hearted effort scatters. Aligned choice plus focused drive gets a project or a career move rolling.",
"major-06|major-08":"A choice about people or values calls for calm courage. Stay steady with a difficult colleague, and hold to what you believe without hardening. Patience and integrity make working relationships that last. Consistent kindness earns real trust.",
"major-06|major-09":"Before choosing between paths or partners, spend time alone with the question. Write down what you value and what each option costs you. Consult one wise person, not a crowd. A decision made from clarity feels lighter.",
"major-06|major-10":"An opportunity or change forces a choice. Timing is partly outside your control, so decide by your values and keep options open where you can. Say yes to what matches who you are becoming, and let the rest pass.",
"major-06|major-11":"A decision has consequences, so look at it clearly. Weigh facts and values, and make sure the choice is fair to the people it touches. Put agreements in writing. Choose what you can stand behind when asked about it later.",
"major-06|major-12":"Decision time arrives, and you are stuck. Rather than forcing it, view it from other angles: what would each option look like in a year? Wait for missing information, and consult the people affected. Good choices sometimes require patient delay.",
"major-06|major-13":"A decision means closing one door. Choose the path that fits your values and release the other with respect. Tidy up commitments, and communicate the change plainly. What ends now makes space for work that suits you better.",
"major-06|major-14":"Good partnerships form through steady collaboration. Blend your strengths with a colleague's, and stay flexible about method while agreed on values. Share the load fairly and adjust often. Balanced cooperation makes the long middle of a project easier.",
"major-06|major-15":"A tempting offer or working relationship may not match your values. Consider whether you are drawn by real fit or by comfort, status or fear of missing out. Look at the fine print honestly. Choose the path that lets you keep your integrity.",
"major-06|major-16":"An abrupt change forces a hard decision about people or direction. Take a breath, gather facts, and choose in line with your principles. What falls apart may have been a poor fit. Rebuilding with better alignment is possible.",
"major-06|major-17":"A hopeful partnership or shared vision is forming. Choose collaborators who share your values, and put your energy into what inspires you. Recovery from a hard patch is easier with good company. Keep your goals honest, and keep them modest at first.",
"major-06|major-18":"A decision feels uncertain because information is incomplete. Hold off on committing until the picture clears. Ask for details, and pay attention to gut discomfort without letting it snowball. Your values remain a steady guide.",
"major-06|major-19":"Working with people you like and respect is a real asset. A choice that reflects your values is paying off. Say thank you, share your happiness in the work, and build on what is going well. Enthusiasm is contagious on a good team.",
"major-06|major-20":"A call to reassess your path aligns with what you value. Review your record honestly, then choose the work or partnership that fits who you are now. Act on the answer with one visible step, such as a conversation or an application.",
"major-06|major-21":"A meaningful collaboration reaches its goal. Choices made from shared values have paid off. Celebrate with the people who helped, and record what worked. Completion is also a good moment to decide what you want your next project to say about you.",
"major-07|major-08":"Drive and gentle mastery combine. Keep moving toward the goal, but manage your temper and your team with steady patience. Setbacks become manageable when you stay calm. Persistent, warm leadership wins cooperation that pressure cannot.",
"major-07|major-09":"Pause the sprint to check your bearings. Take a quiet day, ask one honest question about whether the goal is still yours, and look at the map. Then resume with better direction. A short retreat can save months of misdirected effort.",
"major-07|major-10":"Your plan meets changing conditions. Keep your direction, and adjust tactics as needed. Some things, like market swings or other people's schedules, are outside your control. Focus on effort and readiness, and take good openings as they come.",
"major-07|major-11":"Push ahead while keeping the books straight. Ambition is fine when your methods are fair and your records clear. Own the results, credit others properly, and meet obligations. Progress with integrity holds up when someone looks closely.",
"major-07|major-12":"Progress halts, and forcing it may make it worse. Treat the delay as a chance to reconsider the approach. Look at the project from a different role's viewpoint. When you restart, you will move with better information.",
"major-07|major-13":"A goal or route is done, and pushing on would waste effort. Close it out cleanly, then point your energy at something new. Endings feel abrupt when you are moving fast. Give yourself time to regroup before setting the next target.",
"major-07|major-14":"Sustainable momentum beats sprints. Pace your effort, mix urgency with reflection, and adjust as you learn. Balance workload and recovery. A project moved forward in measured stages usually arrives in better shape. Set a pace you could keep up for a whole quarter, not just a week.",
"major-07|major-15":"Ambition may be steering you. Ask whether the push for status, money or approval is serving you or trapping you. Overwork can feel like progress. Choose a boundary, such as a fixed finishing time, and see what that changes.",
"major-07|major-16":"An abrupt event derails your plan. Breathe, look at what is intact, and decide on the next practical step. Do not push ahead on autopilot. After the shake-up, you can set a more realistic course.",
"major-07|major-17":"Recovery meets forward motion. Point your energy at a goal that inspires you, and keep the pace sustainable. Hope is practical when tied to steps. Ask for support, celebrate small wins, and let your confidence rebuild as you move.",
"major-07|major-18":"Momentum is there, though the map is blurry. Push hard now and you may drive confidently down the wrong road. Get the missing details in writing, check the assumptions behind the plan, and let a trusted colleague test your route before you spend more hours on it.",
"major-07|major-19":"A clear aim and a good mood on the team make hard work feel lighter. Put your momentum into the project people already believe in, and say out loud what is going well. Visible progress now builds the trust you will need for your next request for more responsibility.",
"major-07|major-20":"Your effort is about to be measured, and you may sense a bigger question about where you are going. Review what you have actually delivered, own the gaps, and then choose a path on purpose. Meet the moment with focus instead of defensiveness, and let results speak.",
"major-07|major-21":"Sustained drive is paying off in something finished and whole: a launch, a certification, a project closed out well. Take credit, thank the people who helped, and write down what worked. Then pick your next destination on purpose instead of sliding into whatever is nearest.",
"major-08|major-09":"Calm persistence plus focused solitude is a strong recipe for deep work. Block out interruptions and give a hard problem the time it needs. Come back with one thoughtful answer instead of five rushed ones. Your steadiness will be noticed more than any loud contribution.",
"major-08|major-10":"Conditions are changing around you: budgets, staffing, a market mood. Meet the turn with a level head instead of fighting it. Keep doing the reliable parts of your job well, and stay flexible about the rest. Calm people are the ones others lean on when things move.",
"major-08|major-11":"Firm, gentle handling of a fairness issue gets further than heat. Gather the facts, state them evenly, and keep your tone level. It may involve credit, workload, or a policy that treats people differently. Steady persistence with clear records is your best tool.",
"major-08|major-12":"A stalled project asks for patience, not force. Use the pause to look at the problem from a different side: the client's, the newest hire's, the person downstream. The fresh angle may solve what pressure could not. Hold steady and keep your temper.",
"major-08|major-13":"A role, routine, or team arrangement is finishing. Face it with composure rather than clinging or bitterness. Tidy your handover, keep your relationships intact, and stay steady while the shape of your work changes. What you build next rests on how gracefully you close this out.",
"major-08|major-14":"Slow, even effort wins here. Balance your workload so you can keep going for months, not just this week. Mix up your tasks, share duties, and stay level in meetings. A reputation for calm consistency is worth more than a sprint you cannot maintain.",
"major-08|major-15":"A habit at work keeps repeating: overwork, a difficult dynamic with a boss, chasing approval, or a paycheck you resent. You have the inner strength to loosen it. Name the pattern, set one small boundary this week, and hold it calmly.",
"major-08|major-16":"News or events break the plan without warning. Your job is to stay level while others panic. Sort what is actually damaged from what merely feels shaken, and take the next practical step. Composure now will be remembered once the reshuffle is over.",
"major-08|major-17":"After a rough stretch, steady effort begins to restore confidence. Keep showing up with patience, and let renewed hope guide small improvements: a better routine, a skill you practice, a relationship you repair. Progress may be quiet, but it is real and it builds.",
"major-08|major-18":"Part of the picture at work is hidden, such as unspoken politics, vague expectations, or a rumor. Resist reacting to shadows. Stay composed, ask direct questions, and wait for facts. Your steadiness will help you see more clearly than a hasty move would.",
"major-08|major-19":"Confidence built on real patience is showing. Colleagues feel at ease around you, and your steady handling of pressure has become a strength. Take on something visible, share the credit generously, and let your good energy help a struggling teammate.",
"major-08|major-20":"A moment of reckoning asks how well you have handled pressure. Review your own conduct calmly: what you did well, where temper or avoidance crept in. Then respond to the call with quiet confidence. A fair appraisal, taken gracefully, strengthens your standing.",
"major-08|major-21":"Long, calm effort has ended in a result you can be proud of. Acknowledge what it took: the restraint, the follow-through, the many small decisions. Present the finished work with quiet confidence, then rest before you start a new cycle.",
"major-09|major-10":"Change is reaching your field or team, and stepping back to think will serve you better than reacting. Ask yourself one clear question about where you want to be in a year. Let the shifting conditions show you which door opens, and keep learning meanwhile.",
"major-09|major-11":"Take time to weigh a decision on its merits before anyone pushes you. Read the agreement, the policy, or the feedback carefully and by yourself. Once you see clearly, speak plainly and fairly. Careful thought done alone makes your case hard to argue with.",
"major-09|major-12":"Expect a quiet, suspended stretch: no news, no movement, and time to think. Do not fill it with busywork. Study the problem from a new angle, read, and reflect. When things start again you will move with better information and less noise.",
"major-09|major-13":"A chapter of your working life is closing, and reflection matters more than speed. Ask what you learned, what you would keep, and what you would leave. Take a few quiet days if you can. The next step will be clearer once the old one is truly finished.",
"major-09|major-14":"Thoughtful, solitary focus is best mixed with steady collaboration. Do your deep thinking, then share it in measured doses. Balance heads-down time with conversation so your ideas actually land. A patient, even pace helps you finish what you start.",
"major-09|major-15":"Step away from the daily churn and ask why you stay where you stay. Is it comfort, fear, a title, a paycheck? Naming the hold does not force any decision. It just gives you back your choice, which is where any real change begins.",
"major-09|major-16":"An abrupt change has shaken the ground. Before reacting publicly, take some private time to think. What was really at stake, and what was only habit? A clear, quiet review will help you rebuild on firmer footing instead of patching the old cracks.",
"major-09|major-17":"Reflection is leading to renewed direction. Something you thought through in private now feels worth pursuing. Share it with one trusted person and take a small first step. Modest, sincere effort beats a loud relaunch every time.",
"major-09|major-18":"The situation is unclear and you are tempted to withdraw. Do it wisely: gather facts on your own, separate what is known from what is rumored, and then ask direct questions. Do not decide anything major until the picture sharpens.",
"major-09|major-19":"Time spent thinking has paid off in clear, confident work. You know what you are doing and why. Let your insight become visible, whether through a presentation or a small win. Honest self-knowledge makes you easy for others to trust.",
"major-09|major-20":"A private review has led you to a question about your calling. Consider what you want your work to mean. Then act on it in a measured way: talk to someone, update your skills, or look at different roles. Listen first, then respond.",
"major-09|major-21":"Solitary study or long focused effort has led to real completion. You have mastered something. Let others see it, and step out of the back room. Finishing well means presenting well too, so share the result and plan what comes next.",
"major-10|major-11":"Timing and consequence line up. A recent change may reward your past reliability, or expose a shortcut. Keep your records straight, treat colleagues fairly, and act honorably while things shift. Decisions made with clean hands hold up when the wheel turns again.",
"major-10|major-12":"External conditions move on their own schedule while you wait for a decision. Rather than push, use the delay to consider the problem differently. Update your skills, listen to the people affected, and be ready to move when the wheel does.",
"major-10|major-13":"A large shift ends one phase of your working life. This could be a restructure, a project wrapping up, or a change of role. Do not cling. Finish tasks cleanly, keep good ties with colleagues, and get ready for what follows.",
"major-10|major-14":"Change is under way, and the best response is measured adjustment. Blend old methods with new ones, spread your effort evenly, and avoid extremes in either direction. A calm hand on budgets and schedules helps you ride the turn without losing your footing.",
"major-10|major-15":"A recurring loop keeps coming around: the same conflict, the same overwork, the same money worry. Timing matters, but so does your part in it. Spot what keeps you attached to the pattern, and change one behavior instead of waiting for luck.",
"major-10|major-16":"Big upheaval is here, and much of it is outside your control. A reorganization, a lost client, or a plan overturned. Focus on what is yours to influence: your reaction, your skills, your relationships. The wheel keeps turning, so keep your options open and your reputation good.",
"major-10|major-17":"Fortunes are improving after a difficult stretch. Use the calmer, brighter mood to restore what was neglected: relationships, routines, plans. Do the modest, sincere things that keep good timing working in your favor, and stay generous with colleagues.",
"major-10|major-18":"Circumstances are shifting and information is patchy. Resist making a major move based on rumor or a hunch. Wait for facts, keep asking questions, and hold to steady habits. Timing will clarify itself faster than speculation will.",
"major-10|major-19":"A positive turn brings visible success: recognition, a good match of person and role, or an unexpectedly smooth stretch. Enjoy it, give credit where it belongs, and use the confidence to take on something worthwhile while conditions favor you.",
"major-10|major-20":"Picture a pivotal moment: evaluation, promotion talk, or a clear fork in your path. Review your record honestly, and answer the moment with intention. Timing is helping you, but only if you respond instead of waiting for it to decide for you.",
"major-10|major-21":"A whole chapter finishes at the right moment. Your efforts and good timing combine into a meaningful completion. Acknowledge what you learned, tie up loose ends, and prepare for the next cycle. Ending well opens the door to what comes after.",
"major-11|major-12":"A ruling, review, or negotiation is on hold. Use the interval to examine the case from every side, including the one you dislike. When decisions arrive you will be prepared. Patience with fairness, not pressure, is the wise use of this delay.",
"major-11|major-13":"A role or arrangement ends as a logical consequence of how things developed. Do not fight the outcome, and handle the closing properly. Settle what is owed, be straightforward about what happened, and leave with your integrity intact.",
"major-11|major-14":"A steady, fair way of working is your strength here. Adjust workloads, credit, and expectations gradually so the balance holds. Avoid extremes: neither grand accusations nor silent resentment. Even-handed effort earns quiet respect from the people around you.",
"major-11|major-15":"Check whether the terms you work under are fair, and why you accept them. Fear, habit, or a sense of obligation may be keeping you bound to an unbalanced deal. Read what you agreed to and take a clear-eyed look at your options.",
"major-11|major-16":"Something comes to light and forces a quick reckoning. A mistake, a hidden problem, or an unfair practice is exposed. Stay factual, own your part, and focus on repairs. What gets built afterward needs to rest on honest foundations.",
"major-11|major-17":"A fair outcome brings relief and new optimism. Recognition, correction of an old error, or a more balanced arrangement lifts morale. Use that renewed energy sincerely, keep dealing openly, and plan modest improvements that will last.",
"major-11|major-18":"You have to make a fair call with incomplete information. Do not rule on rumor. Check documents, ask direct questions, and note which pieces are still uncertain. A careful, delayed decision beats a sharp one built on shadows.",
"major-11|major-19":"Fair treatment and clear results line up. Your honest work is recognized, or a dispute resolves in a way that feels right to everyone. Keep being straightforward. A good reputation built on plain dealing is a real asset.",
"major-11|major-20":"A formal or informal evaluation is due. Decisions will reflect real merit and past choices. Prepare by looking honestly at your record, and be ready to acknowledge what fell short. Owning results plainly strengthens your credibility.",
"major-11|major-21":"Fair effort and fair reward meet in a completed piece of work. A settlement, a contract fulfilled, a project closed on good terms. Record the lessons, thank those involved, and let the clean finish be a foundation for what follows.",
"major-12|major-13":"You have been in limbo, and it may soon resolve into a clear ending. Surrender the fight to keep things as they were. Consider what you can learn from the outcome, and close out your responsibilities properly. Fresh direction follows willing release.",
"major-12|major-14":"Slow phases like this one, where nothing seems to move. Rather than pushing, stay balanced. Keep steady routines, do small useful tasks, and think about the problem differently. A calm, even approach lets progress appear when the pause ends.",
"major-12|major-15":"You feel suspended, and something familiar is keeping you there: comfort, fear of change, a habit. Step back and view the situation from outside. Ask what you gain by staying stuck, and whether that is worth its cost.",
"major-12|major-16":"An abrupt event halts your progress. Take time before you respond, because a shift in perspective will help more than quick fixes. Ask what the disruption reveals about assumptions you held. The best rebuilding follows a thoughtful pause.",
"major-12|major-17":"A pause is refilling your energy. Hope returns as you step back and see fresh possibilities. Do not rush to prove anything. Spend the quiet time on learning, planning, or restoring balance, and your next move will feel natural.",
"major-12|major-18":"Stalled and unclear is a tough combination. Avoid deciding anything big for now. Use the pause to gather information, question assumptions, and see the situation from a new position. Clarity tends to arrive quietly, once you stop straining for it.",
"major-12|major-19":"Taking a break or waiting for a decision proves beneficial. A new perspective brings clarity and a lighter mood. Trust that stepping back has not cost you. Return to the work refreshed, with a plainer understanding of what matters.",
"major-12|major-20":"Time on the sidelines has changed how you see your path. Now something asks for your decision. Reflect on what you have learned, and step in the direction that feels genuinely right. A considered response beats a reflex.",
"major-12|major-21":"Patience has finished a cycle. What looked like delay was part of the design, and now the whole picture appears. Take a moment to appreciate the result and share it. Then pick your next move consciously.",
"major-13|major-14":"Endings call for a careful transition. Blend the old with the new gradually: hand over duties, train replacements, and pace yourself. Avoid extremes in either direction. A balanced rebuild is more durable than a sudden overhaul.",
"major-13|major-15":"A necessary ending is met with resistance from your own attachments: security, status, routine. Ask what you are afraid of losing. Once you name it, the release becomes easier, and moving on stops feeling impossible.",
"major-13|major-16":"Major, abrupt endings can shake your working life. Be gentle with yourself as you process it. Attend to practical needs first: communication, documents, next steps. The cleared ground is an opportunity, though it may not feel like one yet.",
"major-13|major-17":"Following a closure, a hopeful new direction appears. Let the end of one phase leave you free to pursue what you truly care about. Take small, sincere steps. Optimism plus a clean slate is a good foundation.",
"major-13|major-18":"A transition has left the future unclear. Accept that you cannot see the whole route yet. Keep to simple daily tasks, gather information, and avoid drastic choices while the fog remains. Direction will emerge as you move slowly.",
"major-13|major-19":"A finished phase gives way to something brighter. Letting go of an old role or habit has energized you. Share your enthusiasm, and take on work that suits your renewed confidence. Positive change is under way.",
"major-13|major-20":"A career chapter closes with an evaluation, and something new calls. Review what you have done honestly, then step into a different way of working. Transformation follows when you accept both the ending and the summons.",
"major-13|major-21":"A major stage finishes, and you can see what you built. Celebrate the result while releasing the old role. Document what you learned and pass it on. Endings that come with completion are a strong base for the next step.",
"major-14|major-15":"Extremes are tempting, like overwork or avoidance, and both cost you. Find a sustainable rhythm. Set a small boundary, keep it, and adjust gradually. Steady balance loosens the grip of habits that quick fixes never reach.",
"major-14|major-16":"Sudden trouble tests your balance. Keep your head, and avoid overcorrecting. Handle the urgent matters first, then adjust plans gradually. Your calm will help colleagues, and a patient rebuild will hold better than a rushed one.",
"major-14|major-17":"Calm and hopeful, this phase is one where progress comes in steady doses. Keep practicing, learning, and adjusting. Do not expect overnight results. A patient routine, honest self-belief, and modest goals build lasting confidence. Write down one small improvement each week so you can see how far you have come.",
"major-14|major-18":"Ambiguity calls for measured responses. Avoid big moves while details are unclear. Balance caution with steady progress on the tasks you do understand. Keep your communication even, and let clarity arrive by degrees. Put questions in writing and wait for answers before you commit.",
"major-14|major-19":"A comfortable, productive rhythm has arrived. Your effort feels balanced, and people are pleased. Protect it: keep regular hours, share credit, and avoid overloading yourself. Sustainable good results are worth more than intense bursts. When someone asks for more, check the calendar before you say yes.",
"major-14|major-20":"Give a thoughtful review to how you have balanced things. Look at workload, relationships, and priorities, and make calm adjustments. If a decision is called for, weigh it steadily rather than reacting. A moderate, informed step is right.",
"major-14|major-21":"Steady, balanced effort has reached a satisfying finish. The consistency paid off. Enjoy the result and note the methods that worked. Carry that measured approach into the next project, and share it with newer colleagues.",
"major-15|major-16":"A restrictive situation breaks open. A job, arrangement, or habit that trapped you falls apart, perhaps not on your terms. Focus on stability first, then recognize the freedom in it. Plan next steps with clear eyes.",
"major-15|major-17":"There is a way out of the rut, and you can see it. Hope helps you break a discouraging routine or an unhealthy pull, like overwork or people-pleasing. Choose one small change today. Encouragement and steady effort do the rest.",
"major-15|major-18":"Uncertainty makes it easy to stay in a bad arrangement, and worry can exaggerate the risks. Write down what you know and what you only fear. Ask directly for information. Decisions made in less fog will feel more free.",
"major-15|major-19":"Energy and clarity show a way out of a tight spot. What once felt like an obligation now looks optional. Move toward work that brings visible, honest satisfaction. Freedom arrives as you notice what you are actually happy doing.",
"major-15|major-20":"You are being asked to face what has kept you tied to a situation, and to answer. Review your commitments honestly. Choosing differently is possible, and the reckoning may be the very thing that frees you.",
"major-15|major-21":"A long entanglement reaches completion. You have outgrown a role or a constraint, and the door is open. Finish cleanly, acknowledge what you gained, and choose the next phase deliberately, not out of habit. A short note of thanks to the people who helped along the way closes the chapter well.",
"major-16|major-17":"After a shake-up comes renewal. The disruption has cleared the way, and you can rebuild with a lighter heart. Start with honest, small steps. Encourage colleagues, and take the chance to redesign what did not work.",
"major-16|major-18":"Upheaval plus unclear information is stressful. Avoid reacting to rumors and gather actual details. Focus on the tasks in front of you. It will make more sense in time, and steady action beats panic. Keep notes on what you learn so the next decision rests on something solid.",
"major-16|major-19":"A disruption leads to a better outcome than expected. The old structure fell, and the new one works better. Enjoy the relief, be gracious about the change, and use your renewed energy for something worthwhile.",
"major-16|major-20":"A shake-up brings a reckoning and a call to change direction. Look honestly at what collapsed and what you contributed. Respond to the moment decisively but thoughtfully, and let it lead toward more authentic work.",
"major-16|major-21":"A sudden change closes a major chapter. Although unexpected, it completes a cycle. Wrap up what you can, acknowledge what you achieved, and prepare for the new phase. You bring your experience with you. A short list of what worked and what did not will make the next start easier.",
"major-17|major-18":"Optimism meets ambiguity. Keep your goals in view even when details are unclear. Take small steps, ask questions, and trust your sense of direction while verifying facts. Patience helps you find your path. Checking one assumption a day keeps hope honest.",
"major-17|major-19":"Recognition, optimism, and clear success come together. Your sincere efforts are visible and appreciated. Share your good spirits with the team, and keep aiming at goals that matter to you. Confidence is well earned now.",
"major-17|major-20":"A renewed sense of purpose responds to a summons. You are evaluating your path and feel drawn toward meaningful work. Act on it in small, honest ways: learn something new, talk to mentors, refine your goals.",
"major-17|major-21":"Faith and effort have finished a significant piece of work. You have earned real satisfaction. Take a day to look back at how far the project came. Celebrate sincerely, thank your supporters, and let your renewed confidence guide your next ambition.",
"major-18|major-19":"Confusion gives way to clarity. Information arrives and the situation becomes understandable, so questions that felt heavy now have simple answers. Share what you learned, own any misreads, and move forward with confidence. Things look simpler in daylight.",
"major-18|major-20":"An inner nudge about your direction is getting louder, though the details are murky. Investigate: ask questions, try small experiments. Respond to the call carefully. A clearer path follows intentional, informed action. Talk it over with someone whose judgment you respect before you settle on a direction.",
"major-18|major-21":"A project is close to completion, but uncertainty lingers. Finish what you started, check details, and do not let anxiety undermine your accomplishment. Clear up remaining loose ends, then celebrate. A final checklist, gone through calmly, will quiet most of the doubt.",
"major-19|major-20":"Success meets reckoning. Recognition is due, and you can review your path with confidence. Answer the call to a more meaningful role, and share your enthusiasm. Clear results speak well for you. Keep a record of those results for the next time you are asked what you bring.",
"major-19|major-21":"Real achievement and real joy arrive together. A significant goal is met, and you are proud of it. Celebrate, credit the team by name, and let the confidence carry into the next challenge without hurry.",
"major-20|major-21":"Reckoning leads to completion. A review or decision has brought a phase to a satisfying close. Accept the results, mark the achievement, and move on with a clear sense of purpose. Take a day to rest before the next thing begins."};
